                <h2>Applicable Scenarios</h2>
<p>This guide applies when your Google Ads account has been suspended due to policy violations, payment issues, or suspicious activity, and you need to submit an appeal for reactivation. The steps below can be followed directly by operations teams.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Identify the Suspension Reason</h2>
<p>The most critical step before appealing is understanding why your account was suspended. Google typically states the reason in the suspension notification. Common types include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Policy Violation</strong>: Promoted content violates Google Ads policies (e.g., misrepresentation, intellectual property infringement, sensitive categories)</li>
<li><strong>Payment Issues</strong>: Credit card rejection, incorrect billing information, or unpaid balance</li>
<li><strong>Suspicious Activity</strong>: Abnormal login patterns, credential stuffing, or sudden traffic spikes</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>How to Check</strong>:<br />
1. Log into Google Ads and check the notification center (top right corner)<br />
2. Click the red "Account Suspended" warning<br />
3. Record the specific policy clause number (e.g., GSP 2.2 or Brand Trademark Policy)<br />
4. Screenshot the entire notification email as supporting documentation</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Tip</strong>: If the notification is vague, reply directly to the email asking for specific violation details. Google Support typically responds within 24 hours.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Prepare Identity and Qualification Documents</h2>
<p>Depending on the suspension reason, you'll need two categories of materials:</p>
<h3>Basic Identity Proof</h3>
<ul>
<li>Business license scan (stamped with official seal)</li>
<li>Legal representative's ID (front and back)</li>
<li>Brand trademark registration certificate (if trademark complaints are involved)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Industry-Specific Certifications</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Promotion Category</th>
<th>Required Documents</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Financial Products</td>
<td>Financial license, investment risk disclosure</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Healthcare</td>
<td>Pharmaceutical business license, medical device registration</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Games/Apps</td>
<td>App Store/Google Play listing proof</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>E-commerce Stores</td>
<td>Domain ownership proof, return/exchange policy page</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>How to Prepare</strong>:<br />
1. Convert all documents to PDF format, max 10MB per file<br />
2. Naming convention: <code>CompanyName_DocType_Date.pdf</code><br />
3. Ensure all documents are valid—expired documents will be rejected</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Draft Your Appeal Statement</h2>
<p>The Appeal Statement is the core document that reviewers use to decide whether to reactivate your account. Suggested structure:</p>
<pre><code>Paragraph 1: State your appeal purpose briefly
Paragraph 2: Acknowledge the issue (no excuses)
Paragraph 3: Explain corrective actions already taken
Paragraph 4: Commit to future compliance
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Sample Template</strong>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We are [Company Name], operating [Website/App Name]. On [Date], we received notification that our account was suspended due to [Violation Reason].</p>
<p>After internal review, we confirmed that [specific violation details] in our promotional materials caused this issue, resulting from [internal process oversight/third-party agency error].</p>
<p>We have completed the following corrective actions:<br />
1. Deleted all violating creatives and re-reviewed pending content<br />
2. Conducted Google Ads policy training for relevant staff<br />
3. Established a dual-verification mechanism before launching new creatives</p>
<p>We respectfully request your re-evaluation and commit to strict compliance with Google Ads policies going forward.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Important Notes</strong>:<br />
- Send from company official email, not personal email<br />
- Don't exaggerate corrective results—reviewers will verify<br />
- Be sincere but professional; avoid overly apologetic or emotional language</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Submit Your Appeal</h2>
<p>Different suspension types have different submission channels:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Policy Violations</strong>: Submit directly via the "Appeal" button in the dashboard</li>
<li><strong>Payment Issues</strong>: Contact Google Ads Support (400-897-8966)</li>
<li><strong>Sensitive Industry Suspensions</strong>: Email <code>ads-policy-support@google.com</code></li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Submission Process</strong>:<br />
1. Navigate to the appeal page, click "Request Review"<br />
2. Upload all documents prepared in Step 2<br />
3. Paste the appeal statement from Step 3 into the text field<br />
4. After submission, record your case number (format: <code>#CASE-XXXXXX</code>)</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Follow-Up and Secondary Appeals</h2>
<h3>Response Timeframes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Policy violations: Typically 3-5 business days</li>
<li>Payment issues: 1-3 business days</li>
<li>Complex cases: May require 10-15 business days</li>
</ul>
<h3>Checking Status</h3>
<p>Use the Google Ads Help Center to check status with your case number. If no response after the standard timeframe, send a follow-up email:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Subject: Appeal Status Inquiry [CASE Number]<br />
Body: Please confirm the review status of our appeal [CASE Number] submitted on [Date].</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Secondary Appeals</h3>
<p>If your first appeal is rejected, don't immediately resubmit the same materials. Instead:</p>
<ol>
<li>Carefully review the rejection reason</li>
<li>Prepare stronger qualification documents</li>
<li>Refocus your appeal statement</li>
<li>Wait at least 7 days before resubmitting</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Can I get a refund for the suspended account's balance?</strong><br />
A: Generally yes. After successful appeal, request a refund through the dashboard "Refund" section. Unspent balance will be returned to the original payment method.</p>
<p><strong>Q: What if multiple accounts are suspended simultaneously?</strong><br />
A: You need to appeal each account separately. However, if they share connections (e.g., same IP, payment method), resolve the primary account first before addressing others.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Can I create a new account while appealing?</strong><br />
A: Not recommended. Using the same company information on a new account makes it easy for the system to identify it as an "associated account," potentially leading to the new account's suspension as well.</p>
